# Citation metadata for Kaizen-3C / benchmarks.
# Enables GitHub's "Cite this repository" button.
#
# FILL ON RELEASE (when you tag a version + mint the Zenodo DOI — see README):
#   - uncomment `version` + `date-released` to match the git tag
#   - uncomment `doi` with the repo's Zenodo DOI
#   - in preferred-citation, uncomment `doi`/`url` with the arXiv preprint id
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark or the value-add fingerprint methodology, please cite both this repository and the paper below."
title: "Kaizen-3C / benchmarks: value-add fingerprinting for AI software-engineering agents"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Anthony
    family-names: Adame
    affiliation: "Kaizen-3C"
    email: aadame@kaizen-3c.dev
repository-code: "https://github.com/Kaizen-3C/benchmarks"
url: "https://kaizen-3c.dev"
license: MIT
version: "1.0.0"                 # matches git tag v1.0.0
date-released: "2026-05-23"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.20357185"   # Zenodo concept DOI (all versions / always-latest)
keywords:
  - agent architectures
  - code generation
  - LLM evaluation
  - benchmark
  - commit0
  - reproducibility
  - software engineering
  - value-add fingerprinting
abstract: >-
  Library-from-spec benchmarks like commit0 are typically scored by aggregate
  pass-rate, which collapses architectural strengths and weaknesses into a single
  number; two architectures with the same pass-rate may have entirely disjoint
  per-library failure sets. We introduce value-add fingerprinting: a
  per-(architecture x library) measurement reporting value-add over a single-shot
  LLM baseline, the LLM-lean cost ratio, and a per-library weakness signature. We
  apply it across a 10-cell matrix (6 architectures x 2 providers) on 16
  commit0-lite libraries, identifying complementary-weakness patterns, two named
  architectural ceilings on text-based scanning (attribute-access invisibility,
  relative-import resolution failure), and a 31x cost gap traceable to scaffold
  trajectory length rather than caching. We release the methodology, the 10-cell
  reference matrix, and a reproducibility package under MIT, reproducible
  end-to-end at $247.53.
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: "Kaizen-3C: Value-Add Fingerprinting for Library-from-Spec Agent Architectures"
  authors:
    - given-names: Anthony
      family-names: Adame
      affiliation: "Kaizen-3C"
      email: aadame@kaizen-3c.dev
  year: 2026
  notes: "Preprint forthcoming; update with the arXiv/Zenodo identifier on release."
